well let's get reaction to that report's  findings general david petraeus the  former commander of the isaf the  international military mission in  afghanistan and former director of the  cia joins us now from arlington in  virginia general petraeus good to see  you this afternoon thank you for joining  us here on sky news  good to be with you sir jane thank you  um you'll have had a chance to have a  look at the findings of this report  published here in the uk about the uk's  withdrawal from afghanistan summed up as  a disaster and a betrayal from what you  witnessed unfold with that uh  withdrawal from afghanistan is that  something you would agree with  certainly i think i would um i'd  describe the withdrawal from afghanistan  as heartbreaking tragic uh and  disastrous  and i think this report captures that  obviously from british perspective and  with respect to what britain was doing  as part of an overall policy that really  was largely determined obviously by  the u.s decision and determination to  withdraw by two consecutive  administrations in washington  uh i found the report really quite in a  way characteristically british though  it's it's forthright uh it's factual  uh it is unsparing  uh and  you know kudos to tom tuckenhut who i  know and to the team and and of course  it's known that i was one of the  witnesses for this  effort if you were to apply those same  principles to the us's withdrawal from  afghanistan and be forthright about that  what would your  personal findings be about the united  states withdrawal from afghanistan at  that time  well again the bottom line is that the  ultimate  effect i think was disastrous it  condemned a country of some 40 million  citizens whom we sought to help for 20  years at considerable uh sacrifice both  in the part of coalition  members and also afghans in fact  mostly afghans keep in mind that these  accusations that the afghans wouldn't  fight were just unfounded there 26 or 27  times as many afghan soldiers were  killed  as were american soldiers for example  um i said when the decision was  announced that i feared that we would  come to regret this decision and i think  that we have  the decision and also the way withdrawal  was conducted also allowed potential  adversaries to  say that the u.s is not a dependable  partner and a great power in decline now  i don't believe in either of those  and i believe that the us and uk and by  the way the uk has been walking point in  many respects uh in the response to  the russian unprovoked invasion of  ukraine  but i believe that we are showing  together now together with the rest of  nato and the rest of the western world  that again  uh certainly the u.s can and does lead  the world uh when the interests are seen  and i  am hugely disappointed that we didn't  see that our interests should have led  us to maintain a very modest force  noting that the uk and just about all of  the other coalition members wanted to  stay  and of course were unable to influence  washington to do that  the decision was made  and you feel it it was the wrong one  however there was a lot of time for  nations to be prepared to evacuate from  afghanistan the uk of course being a big  part of that and i just want to pick up  on some of the key points in this report  key failures um they've called them but  one is about leadership something you  know a thing or two about it has to be  said  a top civil servant in the foreign  office sir philip barton was on holiday  at the time as was the uk foreign  secretary and the prime minister  none of them cut their leave short as  kabul fell now the report said this  marks a fundamental lack of seriousness  grip or leadership at a time of national  emergency are you shocked by the actions  of our leaders at a time when  afghanistan a country that the uk and  western allies have been invested in for  so long  didn't take the kind of urgent action  that now they are probably regretting  well i'm sure that they are and i'm sure  that they obviously misjudged what was  going to play out that  should not have been um actually a  surprise uh  i and a number of others a month or more  before the withdrawal  said that i fear that uh there could be  a psychological collapse of the afghan  security forces indeed there's a special  invest  inspector general report out just in the  united states which notes that  two administrations in the us completely  underestimate what the effect was of the  decision to withdraw when it was  announced  on the afghans both afghan military and  police and and afghans in government as  well there was just a complete lack of  appreciation  uh for what the impact of that was on  those who we were depending to continue  to fight uh and noting that of course  one of the really overlooked elements of  this was not just that we withdrew 3 500  u.s forces or less actually 8 500  coalition forces  we also withdrew over 15 000 contractors  western contractors who were needed  desperately to maintain the most  important element in the afghan military  which was the air component particularly  the helicopters  that are relied upon to bring the  reinforcements out to any one of the  afghan  elements around the country who are  under attack and these are quite good  forces about 3 500 commandos  comprise that reserve  and the key was the u.s blackhawk  helicopters that would ferry them out  there and also provide emergency  resupply air medivac and also then there  was close air support uh as another  element of that yeah  actually i'm quite cl pleased that  you've put that context to the situation  there because one of the excuses we kept  hearing after  the the fall of kabul is that the speed  of the takeover of the taliban couldn't  have been anticipated it took everyone  by surprise do you buy that as an excuse  no i i think it could have been and  should have been again  as i said i did state publicly and i was  a bit cautious with my words  and  was all along and again by the way i  don't do domestic politics so don't ask  me what should happen to the individuals  in the foreign office or something like  that i don't do that here either  but in terms of this policy  uh i think there was a complete lack of  appreciation for the effect on  all of these entities in afghanistan uh  frankly we now know that the taliban  once they knew that we were leaving  started to negotiate with those on the  front lines and once the front lines  realize nobody's coming to the rescue  why should they continue to fight  afghans are survivors they have seen  different countries forces wash across  them numerous times of course british  forces among them  on many  quite famous occasions  and in this case we just completely  missed that the other big error here was  conducting a withdrawal at the height of  the fighting season so the conditions  could not have been more propitious for  the taliban and the haqqani network and  the other elements of their particular  opposition force  uh to make the attacks and to then march  to kabul um so again there were just  enormous misjudgments and  miscalculations and again failure to  appreciate  uh the timing of this you know why not  do it in the middle of winter when at  least it's snowing and it's hard to get  to kabul  and you might have some chance by the  way there are a number of failings on  the afghan side as well the special  inspector general report for afghanistan  notes as an example that  then president ashraf ghani fired all of  his senior military in the final weeks  and replaced them with older ones that  he felt in whom he had more confidence  but basically from the communist era you  know that's an enormous blow to morale  in the ranks if you see all those who  have earned their way into these  positions over many years of very tough  fighting all of a sudden swept aside  because the president doesn't have con  confidence in them and also a failure to  craft a true  uh defensive concept for the country  which should have acknowledged that okay  we can't defend everything  but by golly we can defend these and  these will be our priority and this is  how we're going to do this and if  necessary you know the kind of zelinski  leadership that has been positively  churchillian in the case of uh ukraine  uh was completely lacking obviously in  kabul where all the intrigue and  scheming and all the rest of that had  been debilitating for many years  but was particularly so in this  particular situation general petraeus  i'm conscious we have limited time with  you and there is so much to talk about  when it comes to  afghanistan we can't possibly cover it  all but i do want to talk to you about  duty of care another key failure found  in this report  was that  afghan allies afghan nationals that  helped the west um during the war were  let down there was a shocking absence of  a plan to help them i mean you'll have  worked with many of them you'll have  looked in their eyes when you work with  them and you know i know many promises  were made to look after them because  they helped the western sadly for so  many we talk about the afghans being  survivors many of them sadly haven't  survived because they were left behind  by the west i just wanted to get your  reflection on that  well what you're describing is a moral  obligation uh and we have not met that  moral obligation uh certainly in the  united states nor in a number of other  countries that's that had forces there  in the u.s we have something called the  special immigrant visa which is extended  to  afghans who serve two or more years now  it's actually been shortened to a year i  believe by a recent change in the law  but again in other words they served on  the front lines as battlefield  interpreters sharing risk and hardship  with our soldiers in many cases they did  many years  on the ground alongside our small units  platoons our son was a platoon leader  there had his terp uh with him during  that period of time and that terp had  already served with several other units  prior to that and served with others  after that they were an enormous  hardship and risk and so forth  and we left  the state department believes that it  could be over a hundred thousand because  we guarantee their families the special  immigrant visa as well as the  individuals who earned it because of  course the  lives are at risk also um  those are still there this is a trickle  that we are getting out and again this  is a real failure uh on our part and i  think that a number of other countries  uh have done the same sadly that doesn't  count at all the hundreds of thousands  of other individuals who  incurred risk by just serving in the  afghan government working to achieve our  mutual goals and objectives  and of course members of their families  and and i'm sure that all of the  veterans there just as i do receive  numerous uh emails and messages through  various means on a daily basis from  those who have been left behind i  support no one left behind which is a  non-profit in the u.s which has enormous  legal expertise considerable financial  resources and so forth to help those  get here and then get their feet on the  ground once they are here  we just haven't been able to get enough  out actually to really do what we need  to do in that regard and so again we  have not met that obligation  to be fair  the president of afghanistan did not  want to alarm people not unlike by the  way president zielinski who right up  until the invasion started uh didn't  want uh certain actions taken either for  fear of alarming the public  um but that obviously was a big  miscalculation as well so their  calculations they've had so many  consequences general david petraeus i'm  conscious that you have to go so i just  want one final question to you if i may  a closing thought if you look at  afghanistan now and the rapid decline  since that final evacuation we're  looking at a country an economic crisis  in the midst of a famine 95 of the  population in afghanistan  going hungry in some respect  is there any future for the west  in afghanistan can they ever work with  the taliban what is the future for  afghanistan because this is a country  that has gone back to the dark ages  since the the west of withdrawal  well i think you've characterized that  very well sarah jane it is the dark ages  it's an 8th or 9th century  interpretation of islam that is beyond  ultra-conservative now  women of course even have to cover even  their faces they're very restricted in  what they can do outside the home in the  economy and so forth so 50 of the  population  is severely restricted girls can't go to  high school much less to college as they  used to be able to do  uh all of this uh not to mention a  variety of other uh of the governmental  policies that  are not going to bring the economy back  needless to say it has collapsed  and i see no prospect of anyone from the  outside be willing being willing to  invest much in fact the  biggest economy biggest elements of the  economy now tragically are those that  surround illegal narcotics  there's a big meth problem there now in  addition to of course the problem  they've always had with with poppy and  opium related uh illegal narcotics so it  is an absolutely tragic situation i do  know that diplomats do and others the  policymakers here are seriously and and  truly concerned about that trying to  figure out how to provide assistance to  those who are most in need through  non-government organizations that could  avoid strengthening the taliban in the  regime and so forth and rewarding them  for their very very again  ultra-conservative actions  but it is an absolute tragedy again and  this is why as i said uh the outcome  there uh has been heartbreaking it has  been tragic and i believe it has been  disastrous  general david petraeus we thank you so  much for your time here on sky news so  good to get your insight and your  reaction to that report on the uk's  withdrawal published today thank you  thank you

